with the war in the ukraine intensifying  many european countries are attempting  to wean themselves off russian gas and  seek other trade partners and energy  sources the government of the democratic  republic of congo is in talks with an  australian energy giant to create the  world's biggest hydropower dam and  provide green hydrogen for a global  market that's a potential energy source  which is climate neutral but there are  questions about where this would leave  local people in the drc whose energy  needs are still unmet  jonas gerdin was in matadi close to  where the grand inga dam is planned and  he sent us this report  matadi in western dlc is the hometown of  glory pansu  [Applause]  he's the president of the country's  association of pharmacists and he's out  canvassing his members  again and again he hears the same  complaint  the lack of a stable electricity supply  a power cut to stop the ventilator  and the cooling system is also down  many medical products  need continuous cold temperatures for  their storage  but the chain of cooling cannot be  maintained and guaranteed  a power cut recently took out these  refrigerators at one of the pharmacies  hemi cell is a blood substitute if not  properly cooled it can cause  complications so it was thrown  why power outages are still a fact of  life for people in matadi  the country's biggest hydroelectric dam  inga is located just a short distance  upstream on the massive congo river  completed between 1972 and 1982 the two  inga dams alone account for 70 percent  of the country's power  most of the drc's power is hydroelectric  but just one in five people have access  to electricity  hamere kawende is service manager at the  dams  the population of the democratic  republic of congo  has almost tripled  and we are now approaching 100 million  people  the power generated by inga1 and inga ii  as they are currently installed is not  enough we need to press on with angular  3.  plans to build africa's biggest  hydroelectric plant have been  circulating for decades  now fortescue future industries an  australian company is in talks with the  country's president  the river congo would be redirected to  flood the bundy river valley storing  water behind a huge concrete wall  the plan has the potential to increase  power generation 25 fold but a recent  presentation explains fortescue's  motivation it wants to use the  electricity to produce green hydrogen to  sell on the world market  apollinari nusoka is the coordinator of  a local congolese development ngo he is  well aware that europe wants to reduce  carbon emissions and dependency on  energy imports from russia he's not  convinced fortescue's offer will benefit  the drc  most of the energy will be used to  produce hydrogen  that's the concept of the center and the  periphery everything that's produced  here will be brought to the center to  the west where they are facing the  challenge of the energy transition  and they think that they need to acquire  hydrogen  but we don't think that we've already  covered the electricity needs of our  country  there is a different view in kinshasa  the capital of the drc  alexi cayembe di bampende  is the special advisor in charge of  infrastructure to the president  he sees the dam as a motor of the  country's industrialization  he has a blunt answer to those who want  to supply electricity to all remote  areas  even if we're going to install  electricity at every  household  if the people don't have a job they want  to be in a position to pay that  electricity so we're looking at a  situation where  that the electricity itself  can  boost  the local economy  back in matadi there's only one thing  for sure for motors of the local economy  like glory panzu  power cuts are a fact of life for  businesses like his  for some analysis of the plans in the  drc i'm joined now by brian comanze an  independent energy analyst based in  south africa and he joins us from  johannesburg ryan welcome to dw news  africa now can we begin by getting your  thoughts on uh the statement from the  president's special  advisor he says the plan is not  intended to supply electricity to people  in the whole of drc so who is it for  uh thank you yes so there is a increased  demand  for  production of hydrogen  particularly in industrialized nations  policy shifts within the eu in  particular have driven an interest in  investments in the production of  hydrogen as a fuel which may and at a  future point in time replace the need  for lng gas  used for either  mainly for electrification but it does  have some other  applications as well including transport  so  there's not much use not much demand at  the moment in africa for hydrogen so  those hydrogen investments are most  likely  going to be for europe and a particular  drive from this obviously coming as a  result of the the tensions within the  conflict between ukraine and  russia  which has sent gas prices soaring  but essentially  this will be generating power  is there a way that this electricity can  also be distributed to citizens in the  drc for domestic use not just going out  see yes and so the challenge  unfortunately historically has been in  southern africa that  mining  corporations typically multinationals  have  fronted the money or  leveraged governments to put the  investment in for power stations and if  we visualize it like a body they have  put the transportation from these  massive generators that they've invested  in  the arteries go into the mines and heavy  industry uh very minimal heavy industry  outside of south africa  but definitely a lot of mines in drc  zambia among others  and then veins where they do exist goes  into  major towns and cities and largely would  provide electricity which benefits  largely wealthy residents  and and  government buildings and a few  um small industries  so that has been the historical trend  and unfortunately uh the situation drc  it is much the same the grandinger  project has held a lot of present  promise it's a massive infrastructure  project that the potential capacity for  that dam is tremendous but the financial  arrangements that have been structured  um to finance the grand inga have meant  that mining capital has been prioritized  and it's unlikely  we have already seen the communities  have not been privatized it's unlikely  that that will change unfortunately in  the short term  now there are already two inga dams  which are operating well below capacity  what's to say that this third one will  deliver on what it promises  yes so the  challenge of providing reliable stable  electricity that is also affordable to  both residents and industry alike is  very much tied to building a capable  state within the drc  unfortunately  the the two  ingo one and inga 2 projects you  mentioned as well as the aging  transmission infrastructure have  been the result of poor in investment  planning from government and also lack  of lack of regulation lack of  implementation of policy this has been  an issue and also the  the over-indebtedness this is intimately  tied to the over-indebtedness of the drc  um and and  the unfortunate position uh if not uh  almost criminal position it it finds  itself in um historically ever since the  assassination of patrice lumumba  brian can you sum up what a more just  model of power generation would look  like  okay  great so i think uh ingerdam is uh is  actually an an excellent case study  of some of the central problems within  within africa when it comes to  infrastructure finance  um is that at the moment  uh you know a lot of the finance for  anger comes from the world bank and it  has been tied to particular reforms that  have resulted in the liberalization of  the electricity sector in the drc and  yet we see in other parts  of the world in south africa in in in  china in other brazil  um that major rural electrification in  particular you know large part of the  dlc is rural um 